  "summary": "A temporary altar created during Día de Muertos to honor and welcome returning spirits of deceased loved ones.",
  "description": "Characterized by multiple levels decorated with marigolds (cempasúchil), candles, food offerings, photographs, and personal mementos; evolved from indigenous Aztec death rituals blended with Catholic traditions after Spanish colonization.",
  "culturalContext": "The Día de Muertos ofrenda — Mexico's Day of the Dead altar — stacks three cosmic levels with marigolds (cempasúchil), papel picado banners, sugar skulls, pan de muerto, salt, water, copal smoke and photographs of the dead, welcoming ancestral souls home on November 1-2. Pre-Hispanic death veneration fused with Catholic All Souls; UNESCO lists the holiday, and Michoacán's Pátzcuaro cemeteries, Oaxaca's comparsas and Mexico City's mega-ofrenda broadcast the aesthetic globally.",
